  abstract = {Open Source means higher quality, not just some abstract feel-good idea.  It did in 1999.  It will again in 2029.  To make this happen, we need a bit of mild revolution and some evolution in thinking.  If we get back to pragmatic open source roots and remix it with the wealth of what was learned from twenty years of Bay Area startup culture, we're ready to shake things up.

Open innovation makes everything go faster, lets everyone do more with their lives, and it eliminates stupid forms of competition that hurt the consumer.  By looking out for the interests of others, they will look out for us.    Part of that means finding models that embrace specialization and allow the intrinsic economic energy to flow where it absolutely clearly wants to.

We're at a critical time where we have a chance to change the trajectory for the next few decades.  Is it going to be an AI era that is good for us or an AI era overflowing with top-down energy, decreasing mobility, and system that abuses power to make sure the only progress that happens works if the favor of an entrenched few?

Software Engineers currently have a lot to be on the lookout for given the recent behavior in Washington DC and in the Bay Area.  The good thing is that while we might sleep on a lot of things, when the hand that feeds us gets carried away, we have no shortage of bottom-up energy necessary to exercise a little popular sovereignty.  We don't protest.  We just win.

